HolRefute by example
====================

Executable theory examples covering the user-facing surface of HolRefute:
counterexample search over executable definitions, native narrowing,
the Kodkod model finder, the generator and display registries, the
unused-assumption sweep, the extension points, and which half of Refute
serves which library type.

Each file is a self-contained descendant of `refuteTheory`, declares its
theory and ML dependencies, and is meant to be read top to bottom.  The
`upd_expect` clauses make the prose examples executable checks.


Running them
------------

From this directory's parent (`src/HolRefute`), build every example with:

    ../../bin/Holmake examples

To build one example, run Holmake in this directory, for example:

    ../../../bin/Holmake -C examples refuteExample01Theory.uo

The scripts use the standard HOL heap.  Their `Ancestors` and `Libs`
headers load everything else explicitly.

Poly/ML only: the tree build skips HolRefute on other ML systems.

Files 09 and 10 drive the model finder, and sections 4 and 5 of 12 are
model-finder only.  The model finder needs a Kodkodi 1.5.7 component
and a Java runtime:

    export HOL4_KODKODI=/path/to/kodkodi-1.5.7

Without it a model-finder call reports Unknown ("no configured backend").
Two things follow from that, and neither is silent.

Every call in these files asserts its own verdict with an `upd_expect`
clause, so one that degrades to Unknown raises `Refute.expect` rather
than quietly printing something other than what is quoted.  In a theory
build that exception fails the target.  That is the assertion working,
not a fault.

The reach is also wider than the files that make the model finder their
subject.  At default settings NoCounterexample is a model-finder
verdict: the QC backends can only report that they did not exhaust the
search space, and it is kodkod that turns that into "no counterexample
found within the tested finite bounds".  So a call whose transcript
claims NoCounterexample needs Kodkodi even when its own reasons never
mention it.

Measured by running each file with and without HOL4_KODKODI set:

    01  four calls: the NoCounterexample calls of sections 2, 4 and 5,
        and the nitpick of section 8
    02  three calls: two in section 4, and the QuasiGenuine call of
        section 8
    03  one call, the set goal of section 5
    08  the whole sweep: with Kodkodi it reports two theorems carrying
        superfluous assumptions, without it none, counting the
        difference as skipped inconclusive probes rather than raising
    09  every call
    10  every call
    11  three calls: section 3, and both calls of section 4
    12  nine calls: one in section 2, all three in section 4, all three
        in section 5, and both in section 6.  Section 3's model-finder
        call is a tenth that needs Kodkodi but does not raise, because
        Unknown is what it expects either way; only the reason quoted
        under it changes

04, 05, 06 and 07 produce byte-identical output either way.  Everything
else runs with no external tools.


The files
---------

refuteExample01Script.sml
    refute, refute_def, refute_goal, refute_top, try_refute, quickcheck,
    nitpick, REFUTE_TAC.  Goals with assumptions; using the proof
    manager's current goal; branching on the returned outcome.

refuteExample02Script.sml
    Genuine, QuasiGenuine, Potential and Unknown; the certifying theorem
    and how to opt out of it; evaluated terms; several counterexamples;
    timeout, sequential execution, quiet mode, tags, trace levels;
    expectations as assertions; changing the session default.

refuteExample03Script.sml
    Conjectures about your own datatypes, records, options, pairs and
    sets; the size bound and what "within the tested finite bounds"
    means; polymorphic goals via finite types or a default type; nested
    quantifiers.

refuteExample04Script.sml
    NativeSML, Cv and Compute; Auto's priority order and fallthrough; why
    an explicitly chosen substrate reports Unknown instead of switching;
    exhaustive versus random search; seeds and cross-substrate
    reproducibility; per-run statistics.

refuteExample05Script.sml
    Hol_reln premises and Horn-shaped Boolean functions compiled into
    enumerators; fuel via upd_depth and why fuel-bounded exhaustion is
    always reported as incomplete; premise reordering; the same plan on
    all three substrates.

refuteExample06Script.sml
    Function-valued counterexamples as UPDATE chains; partial
    counterexamples with holes; refinement depth; refuting existential
    goals; certification by case-tree replay.

refuteExample07Script.sml
    register_generator with enumerating and random components;
    abstract_generator for a type with an invariant; how a missing
    invariant turns a true lemma into a false-looking one, and how
    restricting generation fixes it.

refuteExample08Script.sml
    check_unused_assms, find_unused_assms, print_unused_assms; reading
    the maximal droppable sets; QC-only probes versus a configuration of
    your own.

refuteExample09Script.sml
    nitpick and the kodkod backend: uninterpreted functions, sets and
    relations; cardinality scopes; reading a model, with types, Skolem
    constants, constants and argument grouping; several models at once;
    models instead of counterexamples; whack, need and atoms; binary
    integers; solver, batch size and threads; what Genuine means here.

refuteExample10Script.sml
    Inductive and coinductive predicates with unrolling depths;
    codatatypes and bisimulation depth; registering a typedef and a
    quotient of your own; display-only term postprocessors; merged
    type-variable scopes; the translation knobs; debug output.

refuteExample11Script.sml
    Registering a backend and its certainty ceiling; what the requires
    and input fields of a backend decide; the refute_simp, refute_psimp
    and refute_unfold theorem sets; ersatz constants; registering a
    substrate of your own; restoring the built-in registrations.

refuteExample12Script.sml
    Which half of Refute serves which library type.  Integers, served by
    both; characters and strings, which only the quickcheck path
    reaches; machine words, and the bare-numeral trap that looks like a
    Refute failure but is HOL's type inference; rationals, which only
    the model finder reaches, through the Frac encoding;
    register_frac_type and what an incomplete ersatz table costs; and
    finite maps as the documented edge, where an Unknown names the
    missing capability rather than judging the conjecture.


Further reading
---------------

`../README` documents the substrate model, the trust model, the full
option table and the known limitations.  `../Refute.sig` is the public
signature: everything used in these files appears there, except where a
file explicitly reaches into an implementation module.  Those are noted
at the point of use, or — where one file has several, as 11 does — once
at the head of the file.

Two entries in that signature are deliberately exercised by nothing here.
`upd_allow_function_inversion` and `upd_use_subtype` are reserved: no
module outside `Refute_Core` reads them, and `show_config` prints both
with a `(reserved)` marker.  There is no behaviour to demonstrate, so
demonstrating them would mean inventing some.  Every other value in
`../Refute.sig` appears in at least one file above.
